(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-06"] = {"7.1":{"s":"Heat and temperature are part of everyday life, from cooking to the weather. This chapter explains how temperature is measured, how it connects to the ideal gas equation, how solids expand on heating, how heat is measured in calorimetry, what happens during a change of state, and how heat travels by conduction, convection and radiation."},"7.2":{"s":"Heat is energy that flows from a hotter body to a colder one because of a temperature difference. Temperature tells how hot a body is, and it decides in which direction heat will flow. When two bodies at different temperatures are in contact they exchange heat until their temperatures are equal. Heat is measured in joule (and calorie); temperature in kelvin or degrees Celsius.","w":["Temperature is not heat: a large cold body can hold more heat than a small hot one."]},"7.3":{"s":"A thermometer uses a physical property that changes steadily with temperature, called the thermometric property, such as the length of a mercury column or the pressure of a gas. Two fixed points define the scale: the ice point and the steam point. On the Celsius scale they are 0 and 100 and on the Fahrenheit scale 32 and 212, with equal divisions between. Thermometers need a calibration against fixed points.","f":[{"x":"T_C/100 = (T_F - 32)/180","n":"Conversion between the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.3, p.117"},{"x":"T = 100*(P - P_1)/(P_2 - P_1)","n":"Temperature in degrees Celsius from a thermometric property P: P_1 at the ice point, P_2 at the steam point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.3, p.117"}],"w":["Fahrenheit has 180 divisions between the fixed points and Celsius 100: the factor is 9/5, and the zero is also different."]},"7.4":{"s":"At low pressures all gases behave the same way, and they are called ideal gases. The volume (at constant pressure) or pressure (at constant volume) of a gas varies linearly with temperature and would reach zero at about −273.15 °C, absolute zero. The kelvin scale starts there. Charles' law, Gay-Lussac's law and Boyle's law combine into the ideal gas equation PV = nRT, with R about 8.31 J per mole per kelvin.","f":[{"x":"T_C/100 = (T_F - 32)/180 = (T_K - 273.15)/100","n":"Celsius, Fahrenheit and kelvin scales compared.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.4, p.120"},{"x":"V/T = constant (constant P), P/T = constant (constant V)","n":"Charles' law and Gay-Lussac's law, with T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.4, p.120"},{"x":"P*V = n*R*T","n":"Ideal gas equation for n moles; R = 8.31 J mol^-1 K^-1 is the universal gas constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.4, p.121"}],"w":["Use kelvin in gas laws: T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15.","Pressure must be absolute, not gauge pressure."]},"7.5":{"s":"Most solids, liquids and gases expand when heated, because the atoms move farther apart. For a solid the increase in length, area or volume is proportional to the original size and the rise in temperature. The coefficient of linear, areal and cubical expansion (α, β, γ) are defined as the fractional increase per degree. They depend on the material. A gas expands much more than a solid or liquid.","f":[{"x":"delta_L/L = alpha*delta_T","n":"Linear expansion: alpha = coefficient of linear expansion (per kelvin or per degree Celsius).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.5, p.121"},{"x":"alpha = (l_2 - l_1)/(l_1*(T_2 - T_1))","n":"From the lengths l_1 and l_2 at temperatures T_1 and T_2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.5, p.122"},{"x":"delta_A/A = beta*delta_T","n":"Areal expansion: beta = coefficient of areal expansion.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.5, p.122"},{"x":"delta_V/V = gamma*delta_T","n":"Volume (cubical) expansion: gamma = coefficient of cubical expansion.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.5, p.123"}],"w":["A temperature difference is the same in °C and K, so α can be quoted either way.","A hole in a plate expands like the material around it, not smaller."]},"7.7":{"s":"Calorimetry is the measurement of heat exchange, done in a well-insulated calorimeter. The heat needed to change the temperature of a body is the mass times the specific heat capacity times the temperature change. Thermal (heat) capacity is the heat per degree for the whole body. In the method of mixtures, the heat lost by the hot body equals the heat gained by the cold body and the calorimeter, which gives an unknown specific heat.","f":[{"x":"Q = m*s*delta_T","n":"Heat gained or lost: m = mass, s = specific heat capacity, delta_T = change in temperature.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.7, p.127"},{"x":"Heat capacity = m*s","n":"Heat capacity (thermal capacity) of a body, in J per kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.7, p.127"},{"x":"m_1*s_1*(T_1 - T) = m_2*s_2*(T - T_2) + m_3*s_3*(T - T_2)","n":"Method of mixtures: m_1 = sample at T_1, m_2 = calorimeter, m_3 = water, all ending at T; heat lost = heat gained.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.7, p.128"}],"w":["Include the heat taken by the calorimeter and the stirrer, not just the water.","Heat lost by the hot body equals heat gained by the cold one only when no heat escapes to the surroundings."]},"7.8":{"s":"A substance changes state (solid, liquid, gas) when heat is added or removed. During the change the temperature stays constant. The heat needed to change one kilogram of a substance from one state to another at constant temperature is its latent heat: the latent heat of fusion for solid to liquid and of vaporisation for liquid to gas. A phase diagram shows where the states coexist; at the triple point all three do.","f":[{"x":"Q = m*L","n":"Heat absorbed or released in a change of state; L = latent heat in J/kg (L_f for fusion, L_v for vaporisation).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.8, p.132"}],"w":["Latent heat of vaporisation is much larger than that of fusion: for water 22.6 × 10⁵ J/kg against 3.33 × 10⁵ J/kg.","During a change of state the temperature does not change, however long you heat."]},"7.9":{"s":"Heat travels by conduction, convection and radiation. In conduction, heat passes through a material from molecule to molecule without the matter moving. The rate depends on the area, the temperature difference, the length of the path and the thermal conductivity k of the material. In convection the heated fluid itself moves. Radiation needs no medium and travels at the speed of light as electromagnetic waves.","f":[{"x":"Q = k*A*(T_1 - T_2)*t/x","n":"Conduction through a slab: k = thermal conductivity, A = area, x = thickness, T_1 > T_2, t = time.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.9, p.135"},{"x":"P_cond = Q/t = k*A*(T_1 - T_2)/x","n":"Rate of heat flow (power) through the slab, in watt.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.9, p.135"}],"w":["Conduction needs a medium and convection needs a fluid; only radiation travels through a vacuum.","In steady state the temperature changes uniformly along the rod, and the rod itself no longer warms."]},"7.10":{"s":"A hot body cools by losing heat to its surroundings. Newton's law of cooling says that the rate of fall of temperature is proportional to the difference between the temperature of the body and that of the surroundings, if the difference is small. As a body cools, the difference falls and so does the rate of cooling, which gives a curve that falls quickly at first and then more slowly.","f":[{"x":"|dT/dt| = C*(T - T_0)","n":"Newton's law of cooling: T = temperature of the body, T_0 = temperature of the surroundings, C = a constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 7.10, p.139"}],"w":["The law holds only for a small temperature difference; it is not a good model for a very hot body."]}};
